<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lüneburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lüneburg is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. Its salt works, active for about a thousand years until 1980, made it one of the richest members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, supplying salt along the Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-salaria">Salt Road</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> for the Baltic herring trade. Long a seat of the Welf dukes, it keeps a well-preserved brick Gothic old town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luneburg.jpg" alt="Lüneburg" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralf Roletschek - Fahrradtechnik auf fahrradmonteur.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010-06-06-lueneburg-by-RalfR-10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry the Lion refounded Lübeck around 1159, opening the Baltic to German merchants and preparing the ground for the later Hanseatic League, and Brunswick and <b>Lüneburg</b>, with its salt works, grew into important towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From Novgorod and Livonia came furs and wax; from Prussia and Poland grain, timber, pitch, potash and amber; from Sweden copper and iron; from Bergen the dried cod known as stockfish; and from the Scania coast of southern Sweden, then Danish, the herring salted with salt from <b>Lüneburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Lüneburg</b> Heath, in today's Lower Saxony, the Norse Great Heathen Army defeated the army of King Louis the Younger.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They were vassals of several overlords and balanced between the bishops of Minden and Osnabrück and the dukes of Brunswick-<b>Lüneburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-diepholz">County of Diepholz</a></li><li><blockquote>Pinneberg and Altona passed to Denmark and the dukes of Holstein-Gottorp, while Schaumburg was divided among Hesse-Kassel, Lippe and Brunswick-<b>Lüneburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein-pinneberg">County of Holstein-Pinneberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts were pressed between the Guelph dukes of Brunswick-<b>Lüneburg</b> and the bishops of Minden and Verden.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hoya">County of Hoya</a></li></ul></section>
